<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Nahj al-Balagha</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1009</span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Nahj al-Balagha, 'The Peak of Eloquence', is a collection of sermons, letters and sayings attributed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ali-ibn-abi-talib">Ali ibn Abi Talib</a>, the fourth <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> and first Shia <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imam">Imam</a>. It was compiled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a> by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-sharif-al-radi">al-Sharif al-Radi</a> and completed around 1009. Admired as a model of Arabic eloquence, it is one of the most important books of Shia <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>, although the authenticity of parts of it has been debated.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nahj-al-balagha.jpg" alt="Nahj al-Balagha" width="320" height="404" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Mohammad bin Ahmad al-Naqib (محمد بن احمد النقیب) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Folio_from_a_Nahj_al-Balagha.gif" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
